WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 122: Andrew Jackson and the Battle of New Orleans: The Miracle on the Mississippi

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.440
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:06.640
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:11.600
[Rebecca] Today we step into the fiery world of America's seventh president, Andrew Jackson.

4
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:20.240
[Rebecca] Long before he transformed the presidency and challenged the American political establishment, Jackson was the Ironworld hero of the Western frontier.

5
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:32.080
[Rebecca] In January 1815, with the Capitol in ashes and the young Republic reeling, Jackson stood in the swamps of Louisiana to defend New Orleans against Britain's finest army.

6
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:40.720
[Rebecca] With a ragtag band of frontiersmen, free men of color, and pirates, he achieved one of the most stunning military upsets in human history.

7
00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.320
[Rebecca] Here is George with the Battle of New Orleans.

8
00:01:02.320 --> 00:01:12.560
[George] A formidable armada of more than 50 royal warships carrying 10,000 elite infantry under Admiral Sir Alexander Cochrane sailed toward the Gulf of Mexico.

9
00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:25.520
[George] These were not raw recruits, they were the legendary Wellington's Invincibles, veteran regiments like the 93rd Highlanders and the 44th Foot, who had chased French Imperial Legions across Spain and Portugal.

10
00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:29.760
[George] Their target was the jewel of the Gulf Coast, New Orleans.

11
00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.040
[George] Strategically, New Orleans was the key to the entire North American continent.

12
00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:43.840
[George] Situated at the mouth of the Mississippi River, it was the sole commercial outlet for the vast territories between the Appalachians and the Rocky Mountains.

13
00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:51.200
[George] Whoever controlled New Orleans controlled the commerce, the food supply, and the economic destiny of the American West.

14
00:01:51.200 --> 00:02:04.760
[George] British commanders privately intended to ignore the Louisiana Purchase entirely, regarding the territory as Spanish property illegally sold by Napoleon, and planned to annex the entire Mississippi Valley to the British Crown.

15
00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:12.040
[George] To defend this vital prize, the United States turned to Major General Andrew Jackson of the Tennessee militia.

16
00:02:12.040 --> 00:02:19.960
[George] Known as Old Hickory for his tough, unbending discipline, Jackson arrived in New Orleans in early December 1814.

17
00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:29.320
[George] He was emaciated from chronic dysentery, racked with fever, and still carried a bullet lodged dangerously close to his heart from an 186th duel.

18
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:43.000
[George] But behind his sunken eyes burned a fierce, incandescent hatred of the British Empire, dating back to his childhood in the Revolutionary War when a British officer had slashed his face with a saber for refusing to clean his boots.

19
00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:49.160
[George] When Jackson surveyed his defensive resources in New Orleans, the situation seemed hopeless.

20
00:02:49.160 --> 00:02:58.200
[George] The city's population of French creoles, Spanish merchants and Anglo Americans was divided, terrified, and rife with rumors of surrender.

21
00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:08.120
[George] Jackson immediately declared martial law, suspended the civil legislature, and began assembling an army unlike anything ever seen under the American flag.

22
00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:23.080
[George] From the wilderness of Tennessee and Kentucky came 3,000 backwoods riflemen, clad in homespun hunting shirts and coonskin caps, carrying long barrel deckered rifles capable of driving a bullet through a silver dollar at 100 yards.

23
00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:38.680
[George] Jackson recruited two battalions of free men of color, free black citizens, French speaking artisans, and Haitian emigres, promising them equal pay and equal bounty lands, making them the first black units to serve under federal command since the Revolution.

24
00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:46.720
[George] He added a contingent of sixty Choctaw warriors under Chief Pushmataha, along with a handful of federal infantry and marines.

25
00:03:44.840 --> 00:03:51.600
[George] Yet Jackson desperately lacked heavy artillery, naval gunners and gunpowder.

26
00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:55.520
[George] For these, Jackson made an audacious pact with the devil.

27
00:03:55.520 --> 00:04:04.960
[George] Operating out of the misty bayous of Barateria Bay was a privateering and smuggling empire led by the dashing, notorious French pirate Jean Lafitte.

28
00:04:04.960 --> 00:04:13.680
[George] The British had offered Lafitte 30,000 pounds sterling and a captain's commission in the Royal Navy to guide their invasion fleet through the bayous.

29
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:25.440
[George] Lafitte spurned the British bribe and offered his men, 36 heavy brass naval cannons and 7,000 pounds of premium gunpowder to Jackson in exchange for presidential pardons.

30
00:04:25.440 --> 00:04:31.760
[George] Jackson shook Lafitte's hand, integrating the pirates into the line as his premier artillery crews.

31
00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:48.720
[George] On December 23, 1814, the British vanguard under Major General John Keene pulled off a daring amphibious landing, navigating shallow bayous to emerge at the Villarray plantation on the east bank of the Mississippi, just nine miles below New Orleans.

32
00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:53.840
[George] A breathless courier dashed into Jackson's headquarters at three o'clock with the news.

33
00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:00.400
[George] Jackson slammed his fist onto the table and swore, by the Eternal, they shall not sleep on our soil.

34
00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:04.400
[George] Rather than waiting to be attacked, Jackson struck instantly.

35
00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:15.440
[George] That night, under cover of dense fog and pitch darkness, Jackson launched a ferocious surprise assault while the American schooner Carolina bombarded the British camp from the river.

36
00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:26.720
[George] In the swirling, terrifying melee of hand to hand combat fought with hunting knives, tomahawks and bayonets, Jackson inflicted nearly 300 casualties before withdrawing.

37
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:31.960
[George] The night attack did not defeat the British, but it achieved something far more vital.

38
00:05:31.960 --> 00:05:39.320
[George] It stunned British commanders into cautious delay, giving Jackson four precious days to fortify a defensive position.

39
00:05:39.320 --> 00:05:51.480
[George] Jackson selected a narrow choke point between the Mississippi River on the right and an impassable Cypress swamp on the left, along the line of an old, abandoned mill race known as the Rodriguez Canal.

40
00:05:51.480 --> 00:06:03.320
[George] For four days and nights, Jackson's men worked frantically with shovels, picks and cypress logs, digging the canal ten feet wide and throwing up a towering earthen parapet four feet high.

41
00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:12.840
[George] Mounted along this line, Jackson were sixteen heavy artillery pieces manned by Lafitte's pirates, Federal Naval gunners, and Louisiana Creoles.

42
00:06:12.840 --> 00:06:20.120
[George] On Christmas Day, the overall British commander arrived at the front, Major General Sir Edward Pecanam.

43
00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:30.920
[George] Pecanam was a 36-year-old aristocratic war hero, the brother-in-law of the Duke of Wellington, celebrated for his brilliant cavalry charges at the Battle of Salamanca.

44
00:06:30.920 --> 00:06:45.160
[George] But Paconam found his army miserable, shivering in water-logged sugarcane fields, pounded daily by American naval guns, and constantly harassed by Kentucky snipers who crept through the weeds at night to pick off British sentries.

45
00:06:45.160 --> 00:06:56.760
[George] After an intense artillery duel on New Year's Day proved that American guns could outshoot the Royal Artillery, Pecanam decided upon a classic, overwhelming European frontal assault.

46
00:06:56.760 --> 00:06:59.880
[George] His plan for January 8 was ambitious.

47
00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:12.760
[George] A secondary force of 1400 troops under Colonel William Thornton would cross the Mississippi in boats to capture the American artillery batteries on the west bank, turning those guns to rake Jackson's line from the flank.

48
00:07:12.760 --> 00:07:22.240
[George] Simultaneously, Pakenam's main army of 6500 veterans would advance across the open Chalmette Plains in two massive assault columns.

49
00:07:22.240 --> 00:07:31.760
[George] Major General Samuel Gibbs would lead 3,000 men against the American left near the swamp, while Major General John Keane attacked the right near the river.

50
00:07:31.760 --> 00:07:42.880
[George] In the center stood the reserve, the legendary 93rd Sutherland Highlanders, a towering battalion of 1,100 giant Scotsmen clad in tartan kilts and feather bonnets.

51
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:51.360
[George] Pakenam was supremely confident that the raw American backwoodsmen would break and run the moment they saw British bayonets emerging from the mist.

52
00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:59.760
[George] Before dawn on Sunday, January 8, 1815, a freezing, bone-chilling mist blanketed the fields of Chalmet.

53
00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:05.600
[George] Behind their earthen parapet, Jackson's men stood shoulder to shoulder, shivering in the dark.

54
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:14.160
[George] Jackson walked the entire length of the ramparts, inspecting the powder horns, checking the cannon fuses, and reminding his men.

55
00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:18.400
[George] Keep cool, aim low, and see that almost every shot tells.

56
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:26.080
[George] At six o'clock in the morning, a British signal rocket screamed into the sky, bursting in a shower of red sparks above the swamp.

57
00:08:26.080 --> 00:08:30.560
[George] Instantly, British drums rolled and bugles sounded the advance.

58
00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:40.880
[George] The main British columns stepped off in magnificent, locked parade ground formation, their scarlet coats gleaming and their thousands of bayonets leveled like a hedge of steel.

59
00:08:40.880 --> 00:08:44.080
[George] Then, disaster struck the British vanguard.

60
00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:53.760
[George] The 44th Regiment of Foot, assigned to carry the heavy scaling ladders and fascines of sugar cane to bridge the canal, had forgotten their equipment in the rear.

61
00:08:53.760 --> 00:09:04.120
[George] By the time they hurried back to retrieve them, the column had advanced 500 yards into the open plain without any means to cross the ditch or climb the American ramparts.

62
00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:13.240
[George] Suddenly, as the morning sun crested the horizon, a sharp river breeze swept across the plain, rolling away the fog like a theater curtain.

63
00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:20.040
[George] There, fully exposed in broad daylight just four hundred yards away, stood the pride of the British Empire.

64
00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:25.480
[George] Jackson calmly mounted the parapet and called out to his pirate gunner, Dominique U.

65
00:09:25.480 --> 00:09:27.800
[George] Lieutenant, you have the range.

66
00:09:27.800 --> 00:09:28.920
[George] Let them have it.

67
00:09:28.920 --> 00:09:32.360
[George] Dominique U blew on his match and touched the touchhole.

68
00:09:32.360 --> 00:09:38.440
[George] The great thirty-two-pound brass cannon roared, tearing a massive hole through the British ranks.

69
00:09:38.440 --> 00:09:42.440
[George] It was the signal for the entire American line to open fire.

70
00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:46.840
[George] What followed was not a battle, it was a localized apocalypse.

71
00:09:46.840 --> 00:09:56.520
[George] Sixteen American cannons, loaded to their muzzles with musket balls, iron scrap and grape shot, fired continuously in deafening salvos.

72
00:09:56.520 --> 00:10:02.680
[George] Simultaneously, Jackson had organized his Tennessee and Kentucky riflemen four ranks deep.

73
00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:10.120
[George] The first ranks stepped up to the parapet, rested their heavy rifles on Cypress logs, picked their targets, and fired.

74
00:10:10.120 --> 00:10:19.880
[George] As they stepped back to reload, the second ranks stepped up and fired, followed by the third and fourth, creating a rolling, unbroken sheet of flame and lead.

75
00:10:19.880 --> 00:10:24.120
[George] The British soldiers walked straight into an inescapable wall of metal.

76
00:10:24.120 --> 00:10:27.400
[George] Entire platoons were swept away in single volleys.

77
00:10:27.400 --> 00:10:30.440
[George] Officers were targeted with ruthless precision.

78
00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:37.720
[George] Within ten minutes, virtually every British colonel, major, and captain in the assault columns was killed or wounded.

79
00:10:37.720 --> 00:10:41.720
[George] General Gibbs fell mortally wounded twenty yards from the canal.

80
00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:50.000
[George] Seeing his army disintegrating into panic, Sir Edward Pakenham rode gallantly to the front of the line, waving his hat to rally the men.

81
00:10:50.240 --> 00:10:56.800
[George] His horse was shot dead beneath him, and as he mounted a second horse, grape shot shattered his right arm.

82
00:10:56.800 --> 00:11:03.840
[George] Moments later, another volley tore through his thigh and spine, mortally wounding the British commander-in-chief.

83
00:11:03.840 --> 00:11:12.880
[George] In a desperate effort to restore order, the 93rd Highlanders marched forward into the killing ground, their bagpipes scurling above the roar of cannon.

84
00:11:12.880 --> 00:11:23.280
[George] But without orders to advance or retreat, the brave Scotsmen halted eighty yards from the ramparts, standing erect in dress formation under continuous rifle fire.

85
00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:25.760
[George] Hundreds were cut down where they stood.

86
00:11:25.760 --> 00:11:32.880
[George] In less than thirty minutes, the British army was utterly shattered, fleeing in wild disorder toward the swamp.

87
00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:38.720
[George] When the smoke cleared from the Chalmet Plain, a scene of unimaginable slaughter was revealed.

88
00:11:38.720 --> 00:11:47.040
[George] More than two thousand British soldiers lay killed, wounded, or captured, including three major generals and eight senior commanders.

89
00:11:47.040 --> 00:11:53.440
[George] Pakenham's body was recovered, placed in a cask of rum to preserve it, and shipped home to England.

90
00:11:53.440 --> 00:12:01.120
[George] Behind Line Jackson, the American casualties were miraculous, only thirteen men killed and thirty-nine wounded.

91
00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:08.800
[George] Never in modern military history had an invading superpower suffered so lopsided a defeat at the hands of a citizen militia.

92
00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:17.120
[George] Ironically, two weeks before the battle, American and British diplomats in Ghent, Belgium, had signed a peace treaty ending the war.

93
00:12:17.120 --> 00:12:24.800
[George] Because news took weeks to cross the winter Atlantic by sailing ship, neither Jackson nor Pakenham knew the treaty existed.

94
00:12:24.800 --> 00:12:28.000
[George] Critics later claimed the battle was fought in vain.

95
00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:29.840
[George] But historians know better.

96
00:12:30.120 --> 00:12:40.920
[George] Had the British captured New Orleans, they would have occupied the mouth of the Mississippi and likely refused to surrender the Louisiana territory, contesting the validity of the purchase.

97
00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:45.800
[George] Jackson's victory secured the continental destiny of the United States forever.

98
00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:52.520
[George] When news of New Orleans reached Washington, it detonated a wave of ecstatic, euphoric patriotism.

99
00:12:52.520 --> 00:12:56.040
[George] The shame of the burning capital was wiped away overnight.

100
00:12:56.040 --> 00:13:03.400
[George] Andrew Jackson became the towering living hero of the Republic, the symbol of the unconquerable American common man.

101
00:13:03.400 --> 00:13:13.480
[George] The road that began in the blood and fog of the Rodriguez Canal would lead old Hickory straight to the White House, forever changing the course of American democracy.

102
00:13:14.040 --> 00:13:21.800
[Rebecca] The Battle of New Orleans transformed Andrew Jackson from a frontier general into a legendary national icon.

103
00:13:21.800 --> 00:13:28.600
[Rebecca] It showed the world that a free, diverse citizen army could defeat the greatest professional military on Earth.

104
00:13:28.600 --> 00:13:33.560
[Rebecca] Next week, we explore Andrew Jackson's greatest domestic trial as president.

105
00:13:33.560 --> 00:13:40.600
[Rebecca] The nullification crisis of 1832, where he stood toe-to-toe with his own vice president, John C.

106
00:13:40.600 --> 00:13:46.120
[Rebecca] Calhoun, threatening to hang any man who dared commit treason against the United States.

107
00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:50.600
[Rebecca] Until then, thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

